{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 3082 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "5084585fb5f3e533f2258fcb911f6e9420f84a2d7314c89cc59c1134f3cd0965" :edb/db-before-t 3081 :edb/db-after-t 3082 :edb/tx-data [[13194139536394 50 #inst "2026-10-17T00:40:31.114Z" 13194139536394 17592186045417 true] [17592186059653 1038 #edb/ref 17592186570010 13194139536394 17592186045417 true] [17592186059653 1038 #edb/ref 17592186570019 13194139536394 17592186045417 true] [17592186059654 1038 #edb/ref 17592186569980 13194139536394 17592186045417 true] [17592186059654 1038 #edb/ref 17592186570013 13194139536394 17592186045417 true] [17592186059840 1038 #edb/ref 17592186569974 13194139536394 17592186045417 true] [17592186059842 1038 #edb/ref 17592186569996 13194139536394 17592186045417 true] [17592186059842 1038 #edb/ref 17592186569997 13194139536394 17592186045417 true] [17592186059843 1038 #edb/ref 17592186570015 13194139536394 17592186045417 true] [17592186059845 1038 #edb/ref 17592186570027 13194139536394 17592186045417 true] [17592186059897 1050 540.0 13194139536394 17592186045417 true] [17592186059943 1050 540.0 13194139536394 17592186045417 true] [17592186059944 1050 420.0 13194139536394 17592186045417 true] [17592186060255 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536394 17592186045417 true] [17592186060256 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536394 17592186045417 true] [17592186060338 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536394 17592186045417 true] [17592186060340 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536394 17592186045417 true] [17592186060429 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536394 17592186045417 true] [17592186060863 1064 "Let's now derive the law of cosines in the following form:\n\n$$\na^2 = b^2 + c^2 - 2bc\\cos A\n$$\n\nConsider $\\triangle ABC$ with sides of lengths $a,b$, and $c$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/7c/7cc2a0cbb6a02af829e070fc54cfdbe616683f86527b119a530053a34048e699.png)\n\nLet's now add the height $\\overline{CH}$ and assume it has length $h$, as shown.\n\n![](images/d4/d4e80b2b54283ea353b687d959e9d8fc6c9303e85081c0a46b48703ee3dae7d5.png)\n\nThe remainder of this proof consists of two steps:\n\n- **Step 1**: Write down expressions for $\\cos A$ and $\\sin A$.\n- **Step 2**: Apply the Pythagorean theorem to $\\triangle BHC$, and simplify.\n\nUsing elementary trigonometry, we have\n\n$$\n\\cos A = \\dfrac{AH}{b}, \\qquad \\sin A = \\dfrac{h}{b}\n$$\n\nwhich can be written as\n\n$$\nAH = b\\cos A, \\qquad h = b\\sin A\n$$\n\nApplying the Pythagorean theorem to $\\triangle BHC$ and using our expression for $h$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na^{2} &= (BH)^{2} + h^{2} \\\\\na^{2} &= (BH)^{2} + b^{2}\\sin^{2} A\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, consider the length of the side $\\overline{AB}$. Since $\\overline{AB}$ consists of two segments, $\\overline{AH}$ and $\\overline{BH}$, we have\n\n$$\nc = AH + BH\\quad \\Longrightarrow\\quad BH = c - AH\n$$\n\nand using our expression for $AH$, we have\n\n$$\nBH = c - b\\cos A\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our expression for $a^2$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na^{2} &= (BH)^{2} + b^{2}\\sin^{2} A \\\\\n&= (c - b\\cos A)^{2} + b^{2}\\sin^{2} A \\\\\n&= c^{2} - 2bc\\cos A + b^{2}\\cos^{2} A + b^{2}\\sin^{2} A \\\\\n&= b^{2}\\cos^{2} A + b^{2}\\sin^{2} A + c^{2} - 2bc\\cos A \\\\\n&= b^{2}(\\cos^{2} A + \\sin^{2} A) + c^{2} - 2bc\\cos A\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt can be shown that\n\n$$\n\\cos^2A + \\sin^2 A = 1\n$$\n\nfor any angle $A$ (this is the Pythagorean theorem in trigonometric form). Therefore, we have\n\n$$\na^2 = b^2 + c^2 - 2bc\\cos A\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nWe can use a similar argument to prove the following results:\n\n$$\nb^{2} = a^{2} + c^{2} - 2ac\\cos B; c^{2} = a^{2} + b^{2} - 2ab\\cos C\n$$" 13194139536394 17592186045417 false] [17592186060863 1064 "Let's now derive the law of cosines in the following form:\n\n$$\na^2 = b^2 + c^2 - 2bc\\cos A\n$$\n\nConsider $\\triangle ABC$ with sides of lengths $a,b,$ and $c,$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/7c/7cc2a0cbb6a02af829e070fc54cfdbe616683f86527b119a530053a34048e699.png)\n\nLet's now add the height $\\overline{CH}$ and assume it has length $h,$ as shown.\n\n![](images/d4/d4e80b2b54283ea353b687d959e9d8fc6c9303e85081c0a46b48703ee3dae7d5.png)\n\nThe remainder of this proof consists of two steps:\n\n- **Step 1**: Write down expressions for $\\cos A$ and $\\sin A.$\n\n- **Step 2**: Apply the Pythagorean theorem to $\\triangle BHC,$ and simplify.\n\nUsing elementary trigonometry, we have\n\n$$\n\\cos A = \\dfrac{AH}{b}, \\qquad \\sin A = \\dfrac{h}{b},\n$$\n\nwhich can be written as\n\n$$\nAH =  b\\cos A, \\qquad h = b\\sin A.\n$$\n\nApplying the Pythagorean theorem to $\\triangle BHC$ and using our expression for $h,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}^{2} & =(BH)^{2}+{h}^{2} \\\\ {a}^{2} & =(BH)^{2}+{b}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, consider the length of the side $\\overline{AB}.$ Since $\\overline{AB}$ consists of two segments, $\\overline{AH}$ and $\\overline{BH},$ we have\n\n$$\nc = AH + BH\\quad \\Longrightarrow\\quad BH = c - AH,\n$$\n\nand using our expression for $AH,$ we have\n\n$$\nBH = c - b\\cos A.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our expression for $a^2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}^{2} & =(BH)^{2}+{b}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡A \\\\  & =(c-b\\operatorname{cos}⁡A)^{2}+{b}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡A \\\\  & ={c}^{2}-2bc\\operatorname{cos}⁡A+{b}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡A+{b}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡A \\\\  & ={b}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡A+{b}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡A+{c}^{2}-2bc\\operatorname{cos}⁡A \\\\  & ={b}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡A+{sin}^{2}⁡A)+{c}^{2}-2bc\\operatorname{cos}⁡A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt can be shown that $\\cos^2A + \\sin^2 A = 1$ for any angle $A$ (this is the Pythagorean theorem in trigonometric form). Therefore, we have\n\n$$\na^2 = b^2 + c^2 - 2bc\\cos A\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nWe can use a similar argument to prove the following results:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}^{2} &={a}^{2}+{c}^{2}-2ac\\operatorname{cos}⁡B \\\\ {c}^{2} &={a}^{2}+{b}^{2}-2ab\\operatorname{cos}⁡C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536394 17592186045417 true] [17592186060882 1064 "Suppose we have a general triangle where\n\n- side $a$ is *opposite* angle $\\angle A$,\n- side $b$ is *opposite* angle $\\angle B$, and\n- side $c$ is *opposite* angle $\\angle C$,\n\nas follows:\n\n![](images/8f/8f3d3bf4824508ef152e8d8d567672d7d61fb36e0ffb533d97be7294e9198956.png)\n\nThe **law of cosines** states that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na^{2} &= b^{2} + c^{2} - 2bc\\cos A \\\\\nb^{2} &= a^{2} + c^{2} - 2ac\\cos B \\\\\nc^{2} &= a^{2} + b^{2} - 2ab\\cos C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can use this to calculate the length of one side when we know two sides and one angle.\n\nIf we look at the law of cosines we see that it's quite similar to the Pythagorean theorem:\n\n$$\na^2+b^2=c^2\n$$\n\nBut remember that the Pythagorean theorem can *only* be used for a *right-angled triangle*!\n\n![](images/db/db9ed4aac9ca46ffc95d393d863cb09e9ba874caf0008410937f961fdf6c7ab6.png)\n\nThe law of cosines is more general and can be used on *any* triangle.\n\n**Note:** To remember the law of cosines, it's helpful to notice that the equations always follow the same pattern:\n\n$$\n(side opposite; the angle)^{2} = (adjacent; side)^{2} + (adjacent; other side)^{2} - 2(adjacent; side)(adjacent; other side)\\cos (angle)\n$$\n\nWe'll show how this formula can be derived at the end of the lesson." 13194139536394 17592186045417 false] [17592186060882 1064 "Suppose we have a general triangle where\n\n- side $a$ is *opposite* angle $\\angle A,$\n\n- side $b$ is *opposite* angle $\\angle B,$ and\n\n- side $c$ is *opposite* angle $\\angle C,$\n\nas follows:\n\n![](images/8f/8f3d3bf4824508ef152e8d8d567672d7d61fb36e0ffb533d97be7294e9198956.png)\n\nThe **law of cosines** states that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}^{2} & ={b}^{2}+{c}^{2}-2bc\\operatorname{cos}⁡A \\\\ {b}^{2} & ={a}^{2}+{c}^{2}-2ac\\operatorname{cos}⁡B \\\\ {c}^{2} & ={a}^{2}+{b}^{2}-2ab\\operatorname{cos}⁡C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can use this to calculate the length of one side when we know two sides and one angle.\n\nIf we look at the law of cosines we see that it's quite similar to the Pythagorean theorem:\n\n$$\na^2+b^2=c^2\n$$\n\nBut remember that the Pythagorean theorem can *only* be used for a *right-angled triangle*!\n\n![](images/db/db9ed4aac9ca46ffc95d393d863cb09e9ba874caf0008410937f961fdf6c7ab6.png)\n\nThe law of cosines is more general and can be used on *any* triangle.\n\n**Note:** To remember the law of cosines, it's helpful to notice that the equations always follow the same pattern:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{pmatrix}\\,\\text{side opposite}\\, \\\\ \\,\\text{the angle}\\,\\end{pmatrix}}^{2}\\,=\\,{\\begin{pmatrix}\\,\\text{adjacent}\\, \\\\ \\,\\text{side}\\,\\end{pmatrix}}^{2}\\,+\\,{\\begin{pmatrix}\\,\\text{adjacent}\\, \\\\ \\,\\text{other side}\\,\\end{pmatrix}}^{2}\\,-\\,2\\begin{pmatrix}\\,\\text{adjacent}\\, \\\\ \\,\\text{side}\\,\\end{pmatrix}\\begin{pmatrix}\\,\\text{adjacent}\\, \\\\ \\,\\text{other side}\\,\\end{pmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\text{angle}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe'll show how this formula can be derived at the end of the lesson." 13194139536394 17592186045417 true] [17592186060884 1064 "Let's look at our general triangle again.\n\n![](images/8f/8f3d3bf4824508ef152e8d8d567672d7d61fb36e0ffb533d97be7294e9198956.png)\n\nWhen we know the lengths of three sides of a triangle, we rearrange the law of cosines to find an angle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na^{2} &= b^{2} + c^{2} - 2bc\\cos A \\\\\n2bc\\cos A &= b^{2} + c^{2} - a^{2} \\\\\n\\cos A &= \\frac{b^{2} + c^{2} - a^{2}}{2bc}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for all the angles, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos A &= \\frac{b^{2} + c^{2} - a^{2}}{2bc}, \\\\\n\\cos B &= \\frac{a^{2} + c^{2} - b^{2}}{2ac}, \\\\\n\\cos C &= \\frac{a^{2} + b^{2} - c^{2}}{2ab}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt's helpful to notice that the equations always follow the same pattern:\n\n$$\n\\cos (angle) = ((adjacent; side)^{2} + (adjacent; other side)^{2} - (side opposite; the angle)^{2})/(2(adjacent; side)(adjacent; other side))\n$$" 13194139536394 17592186045417 false] [17592186060884 1064 "Let's look at our general triangle again.\n\n![](images/8f/8f3d3bf4824508ef152e8d8d567672d7d61fb36e0ffb533d97be7294e9198956.png)\n\nWhen we know the lengths of three sides of a triangle, we rearrange the law of cosines to find an angle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}^{2} & ={b}^{2}+{c}^{2}-2bc\\operatorname{cos}⁡A \\\\ 2bc\\operatorname{cos}⁡A & ={b}^{2}+{c}^{2}-{a}^{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡A & =\\frac{{b}^{2}+{c}^{2}-{a}^{2}}{2bc}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for all the angles, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡A & =\\frac{{b}^{2}+{c}^{2}-{a}^{2}}{2bc}, \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡B & =\\frac{{a}^{2}+{c}^{2}-{b}^{2}}{2ac}, \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡C & =\\frac{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}-{c}^{2}}{2ab}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt's helpful to notice that the equations always follow the same pattern:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\text{angle})=\\frac{{\\begin{pmatrix}\\text{adjacent} \\\\ \\text{side}\\end{pmatrix}}^{2}+{\\begin{pmatrix}\\text{adjacent} \\\\ \\text{other side}\\end{pmatrix}}^{2}-{\\begin{pmatrix}\\text{side opposite} \\\\ \\text{the angle}\\end{pmatrix}}^{2}}{2\\begin{pmatrix}\\text{adjacent} \\\\ \\text{side}\\end{pmatrix}\\begin{pmatrix}\\text{adjacent} \\\\ \\text{other side}\\end{pmatrix}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536394 17592186045417 true] [17592186060905 1064 "A **quadrantal angle** is an angle whose terminal side lies on the $x$ or $y$-axis.\nThe unit circle below illustrates the quadrantal angles that lie on the range $[0^\\circ, 360^\\circ)$.\n\n![](images/79/792a778286533d589a3410223aaf197b07e64ea77566042d7bb8ac13fbad9768.png)\n\nWe can identify four points on the unit circle above,\n\n$$\n(1,0), \\quad (0,1), \\quad (-1,0), \\quad (0,-1)\n$$\n\nthat correspond to the angles\n\n$$\n0^\\circ, \\quad 90^\\circ,\\quad 180^\\circ, \\quad 270^\\circ\n$$\n\nin degrees, or\n\n$$\n0, \\quad \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\quad \\pi, \\quad \\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nin radians.\n\nWe can use these points to find cosine and sine of quadrantal angles. First, recall that a point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta$ using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx = \\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = \\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nFor example, if we are asked to find the value of $\\cos(\\pi)$, then we just look at the $\\color{blue}x$-coordinate of the corresponding point. The point is $({\\color{blue}{-1}},0)$, and therefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\cos(\\pi)={\\color{blue}{-1}}\n$$" 13194139536394 17592186045417 false] [17592186060905 1064 "A **quadrantal angle** is an angle whose terminal side lies on the $x$ or $y$-axis. \nThe unit circle below illustrates the quadrantal angles that lie on the range $[0^\\circ, 360^\\circ).$\n\n![](images/79/792a778286533d589a3410223aaf197b07e64ea77566042d7bb8ac13fbad9768.png)\n\nWe can identify four points on the unit circle above,\n\n$$\n(1,0), \\quad (0,1), \\quad (-1,0), \\quad (0,-1),\n$$\n\nthat correspond to the angles\n\n$$\n0^\\circ, \\quad 90^\\circ,\\quad 180^\\circ, \\quad 270^\\circ,\n$$\n\nin degrees, or\n\n$$\n0, \\quad \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\quad \\pi, \\quad \\dfrac{3\\pi}{2},\n$$\n\nin radians.\n\nWe can use these points to find cosine and sine of quadrantal angles. First, recall that a point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta$ using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx = \\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = \\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nFor example, if we are asked to find the value of $\\cos(\\pi),$ then we just look at the $\\color{blue}x$-coordinate of the corresponding point. The point is $({\\color{blue}{-1}},0),$ and therefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\cos(\\pi)={\\color{blue}{-1}}.\n$$" 13194139536394 17592186045417 true] [17592186060977 1064 "The unit circle provides a convenient way to represent the sine and cosine of special angles in each quadrant.\n\nWe first recall the following:\n\n- The $x$-coordinate of any point on the unit circle equals the *cosine* of the corresponding central angle, and\n- the $y$-coordinate equals the *sine* of the corresponding central angle.\n\nLet's use the unit circle to list the special values of sine and cosine, starting with the first quadrant. First, we mark off the special angles in this quadrant.\n\n![](images/3a/3a0c6eb5bc286272ef67aee5ef0fa625a6f4e44635413ca43060efc9082b1848.png)\n\nNow, let's write down each point's $x$- and $y$-coordinates, corresponding to the cosine and sine of each point's central angle. In addition, we'll highlight the $y$-coordinate at each point.\n\n![](images/68/680c69ce0313fb61addd31be65074211b866ac2dc8ceed8180c34c97169b0fc3.png)\n\nBefore we move on, observe that if we rotate counterclockwise from the positive $x$-axis, we see that the $y$-coordinates at the special angles are given by the following sequence:\n\n$$\nf(n) = \\dfrac{\\sqrt n}{2}, \\qquad n=0,1,2,3,4\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/ae/ae75322c270db13d7ee5ba6079b236b9a18d463f74cc3e5ee70b6fe113d224e3.png)\n\nThere is a similar pattern for the $x$-coordinates (corresponding to the cosine of the central angle). The only difference is that now we go in reverse, from $90^\\circ$ to $0^\\circ$, as shown below\n\n![](images/55/558f15ac17d31bb236fc63474e170ae4790baa8a9bccd375fe866998b4589258.png)\n\nTake a moment to ensure you can reproduce this diagram on your own without looking. We'll add to it shortly." 13194139536394 17592186045417 false] [17592186060977 1064 "The unit circle provides a convenient way to represent the sine and cosine of special angles in each quadrant. \n\nWe first recall the following:\n\n- The ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)-coordinate of any point on the unit circle equals the *cosine* of the corresponding central angle, and\n\n- the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-coordinate equals the *sine* of the corresponding central angle.\n\nLet's use the unit circle to list the special values of sine and cosine, starting with the first quadrant. First, we mark off the special angles in this quadrant.\n\n![](images/3a/3a0c6eb5bc286272ef67aee5ef0fa625a6f4e44635413ca43060efc9082b1848.png)\n\nNow, let's write down each point's ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)- and ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-coordinates, corresponding to the cosine and sine of each point's central angle. In addition, we'll highlight the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-coordinate at each point.\n\n![](images/6b/6bb6bb7cc95a17c605056e1e1e1ae27bc8cf2f124e419c5e6b9810a72b950508.png)\n\nBefore we move on, observe that if we rotate counterclockwise from the positive ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)-axis, we see that the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-coordinates at the special angles are given by the following sequence:\n![](images/ef/ef3998f9651e37dba31e1597a503577243def17e903dbeb5322ab434a4815b3c.svg) \n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/ae/ae75322c270db13d7ee5ba6079b236b9a18d463f74cc3e5ee70b6fe113d224e3.png)\n\nThere is a similar pattern for the ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)-coordinates (corresponding to the cosine of the central angle). The only difference is that now we go in reverse, from ![](images/26/2614c435bff188ded3027f2a8f1ea011b37b41a1d62d2ff4bae181ce9107b5c2.svg) to ![](images/f1/f12b2ec86446e22fc9d1c982c156c7d089a97fecca1670f416ecfd63cf5899d3.svg) as shown below \n\n![](images/2a/2af7f2c7cf14c017a545a4144ed88dffe7b7f7bfb6e13a2b6144552ef3cfaa6c.png)\n\nTake a moment to ensure you can reproduce this diagram on your own without looking. We'll add to it shortly." 13194139536394 17592186045417 true] [17592186060978 1064 "So, we know how the unit circle can be used to list the cosine and sine of the special angles in the *first* quadrant. But what about the other quadrants?\n\nThe values in the remaining quadrants can be obtained from the coordinates in the first quadrant. Let's see how:\n\n- To obtain the points in the *second* quadrant, let's first mark off the special angles in this quadrant. These angles have the same reference angles as the angles in the first quadrant:$180^\\circ - 30^\\circ = 150^\\circ$ $180^\\circ - 45^\\circ = 135^\\circ$ $180^\\circ - 60^\\circ = 120^\\circ$ We mark these angles below and include the quadrantal angle $180^\\circ$.![](images/49/49105a62c4eecffeb32f6777ddf98022c5ca46079a33294e7eeafc9164444cba.png)\nNow, we reflect the coordinates in the first quadrant in the $y$-axis. This changes the sign of the $x$-coordinate at each point and leaves the $y$-coordinate unchanged.![](images/e4/e44980d06903b6570182b99787ca7dec8cceacab2f72dce04fc48b3d7c660059.png)\n- To obtain the points in the third quadrant, we first find the special angles in this quadrant. Again, these angles have the same reference angles as the special angles in the first quadrant:$180^\\circ + 30^\\circ = 210^\\circ$ $180^\\circ + 45^\\circ = 225^\\circ$ $180^\\circ + 60^\\circ = 240^\\circ$ Then, we take the points in the second quadrant and reflect them in the $x$-axis. This changes the sign of the $y$-coordinate at each point and leaves the $x$-coordinate unchanged.![](images/bb/bbfcf14e39ee122cf1bd7048903ac7f205325c77d4b2899c53619495ffeb8d8c.png)\n- Finally, to obtain the points in the fourth quadrant, we first compute the special angles in this quadrant:$360^\\circ - 30^\\circ = 330^\\circ$ $360^\\circ - 45^\\circ = 315^\\circ$ $360^\\circ - 60^\\circ = 300^\\circ$ Then, we take the points in the first quadrant and reflect them in the $x$-axis. This changes the sign of the $y$-coordinate at each point and leaves the $x$-coordinate unchanged.![](images/f5/f507964e8fe06cc54ceab447d570bb004b4e9a2b0b8fa3074762e28a41009a2a.png)\n\nTherefore, our complete unit circle is as follows.\n\n![](images/f5/f5006a5a3c363f83e6d4624fc72fefa140e29ef6ef23db16fbab5538b421a058.png)\n\nTake a moment to ensure you can create this diagram yourself without looking. We'll be referring to it often!" 13194139536394 17592186045417 false] [17592186060978 1064 "So, we know how the unit circle can be used to list the cosine and sine of the special angles in the *first* quadrant. But what about the other quadrants? \n\nThe values in the remaining quadrants can be obtained from the coordinates in the first quadrant. Let's see how:\n\n- To obtain the points in the *second* quadrant, let's first mark off the special angles in this quadrant. These angles have the same reference angles as the angles in the first quadrant:\n\n- $180^\\circ - 30^\\circ = 150^\\circ$\n\n- $180^\\circ - 45^\\circ = 135^\\circ$\n\n- $180^\\circ - 60^\\circ = 120^\\circ$\n\nWe mark these angles below and include the quadrantal angle $180^\\circ.$\n\n![](images/49/49105a62c4eecffeb32f6777ddf98022c5ca46079a33294e7eeafc9164444cba.png)\n\nNow, we reflect the coordinates in the first quadrant in the $y$-axis. This changes the sign of the $x$-coordinate at each point and leaves the $y$-coordinate unchanged.\n\n![](images/e4/e44980d06903b6570182b99787ca7dec8cceacab2f72dce04fc48b3d7c660059.png)\n\n- To obtain the points in the third quadrant, we first find the special angles in this quadrant. Again, these angles have the same reference angles as the special angles in the first quadrant:\n\n- $180^\\circ + 30^\\circ = 210^\\circ$\n\n- $180^\\circ + 45^\\circ = 225^\\circ$\n\n- $180^\\circ + 60^\\circ = 240^\\circ$\n\nThen, we take the points in the second quadrant and reflect them in the $x$-axis. This changes the sign of the $y$-coordinate at each point and leaves the $x$-coordinate unchanged.\n\n![](images/bb/bbfcf14e39ee122cf1bd7048903ac7f205325c77d4b2899c53619495ffeb8d8c.png)\n\n- Finally, to obtain the points in the fourth quadrant, we first compute the special angles in this quadrant:\n\n- $360^\\circ - 30^\\circ = 330^\\circ$\n\n- $360^\\circ - 45^\\circ = 315^\\circ$\n\n- $360^\\circ - 60^\\circ = 300^\\circ$\n\nThen, we take the points in the first quadrant and reflect them in the $x$-axis. This changes the sign of the $y$-coordinate at each point and leaves the $x$-coordinate unchanged.\n\n![](images/f5/f507964e8fe06cc54ceab447d570bb004b4e9a2b0b8fa3074762e28a41009a2a.png)\n\nTherefore, our complete unit circle is as follows.\n\n![](images/f5/f5006a5a3c363f83e6d4624fc72fefa140e29ef6ef23db16fbab5538b421a058.png)\n\nTake a moment to ensure you can create this diagram yourself without looking. We'll be referring to it often!" 13194139536394 17592186045417 true] [17592186083272 1038 #edb/ref 17592186569954 13194139536394 17592186045417 true] [17592186083272 1038 #edb/ref 17592186569987 13194139536394 17592186045417 true] [17592186083272 1038 #edb/ref 17592186570012 13194139536394 17592186045417 true] [17592186083272 1038 #edb/ref 17592186570030 13194139536394 17592186045417 true] [17592186083273 1038 #edb/ref 17592186569958 13194139536394 17592186045417 true] [17592186083273 1038 #edb/ref 17592186569970 13194139536394 17592186045417 true] [17592186083308 1038 #edb/ref 17592186569988 13194139536394 17592186045417 true] [17592186083308 1038 #edb/ref 17592186569999 13194139536394 17592186045417 true] [17592186083308 1038 #edb/ref 17592186570032 13194139536394 17592186045417 true] [17592186083309 1038 #edb/ref 17592186569983 13194139536394 17592186045417 true] [17592186083309 1038 #edb/ref 17592186570000 13194139536394 17592186045417 true] [17592186083309 1038 #edb/ref 17592186570023 13194139536394 17592186045417 true] [17592186083309 1038 #edb/ref 17592186570029 13194139536394 17592186045417 true] [17592186083425 1050 960.0 13194139536394 17592186045417 true] [17592186096761 1038 #edb/ref 17592186570011 13194139536394 17592186045417 true] [17592186096761 1038 #edb/ref 17592186570040 13194139536394 17592186045417 true] [1759218609676